Author: aaron <aaron.hsueh@explorers-residence.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 22:49:38 2026 +0800

    fix: the retimed alpha_power never reached the audio path (C/Python divergence)
    
    pbfdaf_init_scalars() has computed a grid-retimed p->alpha_power since dc31dd2.
    The far-power EMA in pbfdaf_frontend() read a hardcoded 0.9f. The field was
    set, was asserted on by test_rate_structural check (d2), and was consumed by
    nothing.
    
    I introduced this in dc31dd2: that commit retimed Python's self.alpha_power
    (filters.py:291, live) and computed C's p->alpha_power (set, dead), and I then
    verified "C matches Python on all four grids" by comparing the STRUCT FIELDS.
    The fields did match. The audio paths did not:
    
      grid          Python   C (was)   delta
      8000/256      0.844866  0.9      -0.055
      16000/256     0.919166  0.9      +0.019   <- production default
      16000/512     0.844866  0.9      -0.055
      48000/1024    0.893701  0.9      -0.006
    
    Two gates should have caught it and neither could. Check (d2) asserts the value
    an instance ENDS UP WITH, which is necessary but not sufficient -- it cannot
    distinguish a field the audio path reads from one it ignores. parity_pbfdkf.c,
    which compares the actual EMA output against the Python reference, was moved to
    test/historical/ during the release cleanup and wired to no Makefile target, so
    the one gate that compared behaviour rather than storage stopped running in the
    same release that introduced the divergence.
    
    New check (d4) closes the class by measuring the coefficient the EMA ACTUALLY
    applied, recovered from the filter's own state across one hop of constant
    far-end:
    
        a = (power_new - far_psd) / (power_old - far_psd)
    
    It never reads the coefficient, so it cannot be satisfied by a field nothing
    consumes. Mutation-tested: restoring the 0.9f literal fails all four grids with
    the measured value printed as 0.900000 against the expected retimed value,
    while (d2) stays green -- which is exactly the blind spot, demonstrated.
    
    alpha_r has the identical bypass and is DELIBERATELY LEFT DIVERGED, with the
    reasoning recorded at the site. Wiring it would restore parity but would also
    commit both ports to a 16 ms authoring anchor (e9cb383) that has not been
    adjudicated against the alternative 10 ms reading. Syncing a wrong anchor to
    both ends is worse than a visible divergence, because it looks settled.
    
    Verification: rate-structural 284 (was 272), config-validation 160,
    static-aec byte-equal, zero-heap, detectors-parity, process-context all PASS,
    Python 77.

